  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1250 PM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nWith the upper-level troughing still passing through the area, windy \nconditions still persist through the rest of the day as decent\n700mb winds aloft linger. A secondary wave moving through the \narea overnight into Tuesday will push a boundary south across \nNorth- Central MT. This brings along another wave of windy \nconditions with winds of 30-45 mph and very light precipitation. \nThis boundary will also help usher in cooler temperatures for \nMonday. Monday's temperatures are expected to be well below normal\nacross the region, with lower elevation highs only reaching mid \n60s to lower 70s. Higher mountain peaks are forecasted to drop \ninto the 30s Monday morning. Therefore, they could see light \nflurries if they receive any appreciable precipitation. \n\nNorthwest flow aloft settles in through mid week as another upper-\nlevel ridge builds in across the Western U.S. This ridge will \nslowly move to the east and into the area through the week. As a \nresult, temperatures gradually warm to back above normal during \nthe second half of the week. There is not much moisture \nassociated with the ridge, so precipitation chances remain low \nthrough the week and any amounts will be little if an area \nreceives rain. Wildfire smoke will continue to affect the region \nthis week. North winds help push out a good chunk of smoke across\nNorth-Central MT Monday and Tuesday into Southwest MT. -Wilson",
